{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.TMCorrespondence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.TMCorrespondence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.TransferMatrix","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.OptimalNF","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HeckeRep","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.NonsingularInverse","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":40,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.pp_one_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.pp_one_step","kind":"theorem","line":680,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L680-L687","doc":"Sanity check: the initial state `(1, 0)ᵀ` after one pp crossing\ngives `(HA, 1)ᵀ`, meaning the Hecke element is `HA · σ + 1`.\nThis is correct: `σ · σ = σ² = HA · σ + 1` by the Hecke relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.transfer_pp_correct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.transfer_pp_correct","kind":"theorem","line":672,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L672-L678","doc":"Sanity check: the transfer matrix for `(c, d) = (1, 0)` is `T_pp`.\nMultiplying a Hecke element by `(1·σ + 0)` just appends a generator,\ncorresponding to a pure proton crossing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.skein_from_markov_traces","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.skein_from_markov_traces","kind":"theorem","line":647,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L647-L666","doc":"The skein polynomial equals the product of per-generator Markov traces.\n\n`P(q) = ∏_{j=0}^{A-2} V̂_j`\n\nwhere each `V̂_j` is the Markov trace of the j-th Hecke element.\nIn cleared form:\n\n`(t+t⁻¹)^{A-1} · P(q) = ∏_j [a_j + (t+t⁻¹) · b_j]`\n\nThis follows from `volume_eq_markov_trace` applied to each chain. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.optimal_nf_from_transfer_matrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.optimal_nf_from_transfer_matrix","kind":"theorem","line":624,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L624-L645","doc":"**Corollary (Layer 3 → Layer 2 bridge).**\n\nThe optimal NF has exactly `A` terms (the standard basis) because:\n\n1. Each chain j produces a state `(a_j, b_j)` via the transfer matrix\n2. The Hecke element at generator j is `a_j · σ_j + b_j · 𝟏`\n   (by `chain_correspondence`)\n3. The total element is `b₀ · 𝟏 + ∑_j a_j · σ_j` (by linearity)\n4. This has support ⊆ {𝟏, σ₀, σ₁, …, σ_{A-2}} = standardBasis(A-1)\n5. Under valley of stability (N ≥ Z-1), all `a_j ≠ 0` (the crossing\n   coefficient is nonzero), so the support equals the standard basis\n\nThe NF has `A` terms: one identity + one per generator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_buildChains","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_buildChains","kind":"theorem","line":596,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L596-L617","doc":"The matrix chain from `chainCrossings` equals the corresponding\nentry in `buildChains`.\n\nThis is the bridge between the abstract crossing-sequence formulation\nand the concrete inductive chain construction in TransferMatrix.lean.\n\n**PROVED 2026-09-05 — the class hypothesis is gone.**  This declaration\nused to carry `[MatrixChainEqBuildChains config j hj]` and pass straight\nthrough to that class's field.  It no longer does: both sides are shown\nto be the ordered `crossingMatrix` product over the nucleons after `j`.\n\nThe old four-step proof sketch that stood here was right about step 1\n(`crossingMatrix_eq`) and wrong about the rest.  There was no\n`foldl`-versus-`foldr` obstacle: `matrixChain` *reverses* its list, which\n`matrixChain_eq_prod` handles via `List.Perm.prod_eq'`, and the real work\nwas the `buildChains` accumulator invariant (`foldl_take`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_buildChains_axiom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_buildChains_axiom","kind":"theorem","line":582,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L582-L594","doc":"Per-instance form, kept for compatibility.\n\n**The name is now a misnomer and the disclosure it used to carry is\nwithdrawn**: this is no longer an axiom pass-through, because the class\nis proved (`instMatrixChainEqBuildChains`).  Prefer\n`matrixChain_eq_buildChains_unconditional`.  Retained so that any caller\nwritten against the conditional API keeps working. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.instMatrixChainEqBuildChains","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.instMatrixChainEqBuildChains","kind":"instance","line":576,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L576-L580","doc":"The conjecture class is inhabited — it was never more than a placeholder\nfor the theorem above. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_buildChains_unconditional","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_buildChains_unconditional","kind":"theorem","line":568,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L568-L574","doc":"**The statement, unconditionally.**  No class hypothesis, no instance,\nno `sorry`.  Both sides are the ordered `crossingMatrix` product over the\nnucleons after `j`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.MatrixChainEqBuildChains.eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.MatrixChainEqBuildChains.eq","kind":"theorem","line":565,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L565-L565","doc":"foldl and foldr agree on the commutative chain product. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.MatrixChainEqBuildChains.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.MatrixChainEqBuildChains.mk","kind":"ctor","line":562,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L562-L562","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.MatrixChainEqBuildChains","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.MatrixChainEqBuildChains","kind":"class","line":516,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L516-L566","doc":"**foldl/foldr bridge — typeclass (Pattern 4).**\n\nThe universal claim\n`matrixChain (chainCrossings config j) = (buildChains config).get! j`\nholds because all transfer matrices commute (so `foldl` and\n`foldr` agree), and callers witness the equality for the specific\n`(config, j)` pair they use.\n\n**The fold-direction framing was wrong twice; corrected 2026-09-05\n(second pass, measured).**\n\nThe original text cited `List.foldl_eq_foldr_of_comm` as *\"not yet\navailable\"*.  No lemma of that name exists.  The first correction, made\nthe same day, replaced it with *\"what is missing is a pointwise-commuting\nvariant without the `flip`\"*.  **That was also wrong**, and in the more\nexpensive direction: it left a closeable sub-problem looking open and\nstill mislocated the real one.\n\nWhat is actually true, now proved above:\n\n* `matrixChain` has **no fold-direction problem**.  Its recursion\n  reverses the list, and reversal under pairwise commutation is\n  `List.Perm.prod_eq'` + `List.reverse_perm` — both already in Mathlib.\n  `matrixChain_eq_prod` discharges it outright, sorry-free, with no\n  instance and no `flip`.\n* `QOU.MathlibExt` §3a's `foldl_eq_foldr_of_commute_init` was\n  documented as discharging this class \"after some rearrangement\".  It\n  does not: it relocates the *accumulator*, leaving list order alone,\n  which is not the operation needed here.  Corrected there too.\n\n**THE GAP IS CLOSED (2026-09-05).  This class is proved and has an\ninstance — `instMatrixChainEqBuildChains` below.**  It is kept as the\nrecord of the route, not because anything is still assumed.\n\nThe two halves, each sorry-free and neither needing an instance:\n\n* **Left** — `matrixChain_chainCrossings`: `matrixChain (chainCrossings\n  config j)` is the ordered `crossingMatrix` product over the nucleons\n  after `j`.  Built from `matrixChain_eq_prod` (the reversal) and\n  `crossingMatrix_eq` (the coefficients).\n* **Right** — `buildChains_get`: chain `j` of `buildChains` is *the same\n  product*.  Built from `buildChains_eq_foldl` (the `[]` / `[_]` arms are\n  redundant, so no case split survives) and `foldl_take` (the accumulator\n  invariant, by induction on the prefix length via `chainsUpTo_succ`).\n\nNothing new was needed from Mathlib at any point. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.buildChains_get","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.buildChains_get","kind":"theorem","line":501,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L501-L514","doc":"**The right-hand side of `MatrixChainEqBuildChains`.**  Chain `j` of\n`buildChains` is the ordered `crossingMatrix` product over the nucleons\nafter `j`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.foldl_take","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.foldl_take","kind":"theorem","line":483,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L483-L499","doc":"**The accumulator invariant.**  Folding a prefix of length `m` leaves the\ncounter at `m` and the chain list at `chainsUpTo config m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.chainsUpTo_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.chainsUpTo_succ","kind":"theorem","line":459,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L459-L481","doc":"One fold step on `chainsUpTo`: every existing chain gains the new\nnucleon's crossing, and one new chain is appended. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.chainsUpTo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.chainsUpTo","kind":"def","line":452,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L452-L457","doc":"The chain list after `buildChains` has folded the first `m` nucleons:\nchain `j` is the ordered `crossingMatrix` product over the nucleons\nstrictly between `j` and `m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.take_succ_of_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.take_succ_of_lt","kind":"theorem","line":444,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L444-L450","doc":"In-range `take` peel, with the partial accessor `config[m]!` resolved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.mapIdx_map_range","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.mapIdx_map_range","kind":"theorem","line":437,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L437-L442","doc":"`mapIdx` over a mapped `range` is a `map` of the two composed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.buildChains_eq_foldl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.buildChains_eq_foldl","kind":"theorem","line":416,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L416-L435","doc":"**`buildChains` is its own fold — unconditionally, on all three arms.**\n\n`buildChains` is written with `[]` and `[_]` special-cased before a\ncatch-all, which makes the catch-all's equation lemma carry\n\"not nil and not singleton\" side conditions and is why this looked like\nit needed a three-way case split to reason about.\n\nIt does not: the two special cases are **redundant**. Folding `[]` gives\n`([], 0)` and folding `[x]` gives `([], 1)` — first component `[]` either\nway, exactly what those arms return. So the fold form holds for every\nconfiguration and `rfl` discharges all three branches.\n\nThis removes the side conditions from any induction over `buildChains`;\nwhat remains is the accumulator invariant (bean `qou-affg`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.buildStep","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.buildStep","kind":"def","line":397,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L397-L414","doc":"The single accumulator step inside `buildChains`, named so the fold can\nbe reasoned about.\n\n**The body is term-identical to `buildChains`'s step on purpose**, including\nthe deprecated `List.get!` (which is why this def inherits that deprecation\nwarning). `buildChains_eq_foldl` below is proved by `rfl`, and modernising\nthis one call to `config[k - 1]!` breaks it — measured: `rfl` fails, the two\naccessors are not definitionally equal at that strength. Do not \"tidy\" it\nwithout re-checking that theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_chainCrossings","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_chainCrossings","kind":"theorem","line":371,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L371-L395","doc":"**The left-hand side of `MatrixChainEqBuildChains`, with all matrix\nalgebra eliminated.**\n\nCombining `matrixChain_eq_prod` (the reversal) with `crossingMatrix_eq`\n(the coefficients), the chain-`j` matrix is literally the ordered product\nof `crossingMatrix` over the nucleons after `j` — which is the object\n`buildChains` assembles.\n\nConsequence, and the point of this lemma: `MatrixChainEqBuildChains` is\nnow equivalent to the purely combinatorial\n\n  `(buildChains config).get! j = ((config.drop (j+1)).map`\n  `  (fun nk => crossingMatrix config[j]! nk)).prod`\n\ncarrying no Hecke, transfer-matrix or fold-direction content whatsoever.\nSee that class's docstring; the remainder is tracked as bean `qou-affg`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.crossingMatrix_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.crossingMatrix_eq","kind":"theorem","line":348,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L348-L369","doc":"**`crossingMatrix` IS the transfer matrix at the coefficients\n`chainCrossings` emits** — step 1 of the four-step proof sketch on\n`matrixChain_eq_buildChains`, now discharged.\n\nThe doubling convention lines up in all three vertex cases:\n`pp` gives `2 • T(1,0) = T(2,0)`, `nn` gives `2 • T(1,−HA) = T(2,−2HA)`,\nand `pn`'s `two_T_pn` is already `T(2,−HA)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.smul_transferMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.smul_transferMatrix","kind":"theorem","line":340,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L340-L346","doc":"The transfer matrix is linear in its coefficient pair:\n`n • T(c, d) = T(n·c, n·d)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_prod","kind":"theorem","line":317,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L317-L334","doc":"**The reversal is harmless: `matrixChain` is the forward product.**\n\nProved, sorry-free, depending on no class instance: `transferMatrix_comm`\n(a theorem since 2026-06-07) makes any two transfer matrices commute, so\n`QOU.MathlibExt.prod_reverse_eq_of_pairwise_commute` removes the reversal\nintroduced by the recursion.\n\nThis is the whole of the \"different association order\" half of\n`MatrixChainEqBuildChains`, and it is **not** conditional on anything.\nWhat remains open there is a different question entirely — see that\nclass's docstring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_reverse_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain_eq_reverse_prod","kind":"theorem","line":302,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L302-L315","doc":"`matrixChain` is the **reversed** product of its per-crossing transfer\nmatrices.\n\nImmediate from the defining recursion `matrixChain ((c,d) :: rest) =\nmatrixChain rest * transferMatrix c d`, which puts the *first* crossing\nof the sequence *rightmost* in the product. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.chainCrossings","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.chainCrossings","kind":"def","line":276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L276-L300","doc":"Extract the crossing sequence for chain j from a nuclear configuration.\n\nChain j is formed by the crossings between nucleon j and all\nsubsequent nucleons k > j. The crossing at position k contributes\nweights that match `crossingMatrix` from TransferMatrix.lean.\n\n**Scaling convention (v1.1):** pp and nn crossings are DOUBLED\nto match `crossingMatrix`:\n- pp: `crossingMatrix pp = 2 • T_pp` → coefficients `(2, 0)`\n- nn: `crossingMatrix nn = 2 • T_nn` → coefficients `(2, −2·HA)`\n- pn: `crossingMatrix pn = two_T_pn`  → coefficients `(2, −HA)`\n  (the \"undoubled\" pn base would be `(1, −HA/2)`, but\n  `w_plus_t .pn` and `w_zero_t .pn` are defined as the doubled\n  form `(2, −HA)` which already matches `two_T_pn` directly)\n\nThis ensures `transferMatrix c d = crossingMatrix(nj, nk)` for\neach pair, which is needed for `matrixChain_eq_buildChains`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.volume_eq_markov_trace","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.volume_eq_markov_trace","kind":"theorem","line":256,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L256-L270","doc":"**Corollary**: the traced volume V̂_i equals the Markov trace\nof the per-generator Hecke element.\n\nThis bridges the transfer matrix world (TransferMatrix.lean)\nand the optimal NF world (OptimalNF.lean):\n- Transfer matrix chain → state vector (a_i, b_i)\n- Hecke chain → NF coefficients (a_i, b_i) [same by correspondence]\n- V̂_i = z·a_i + b_i extracts Markov trace information\n- The skein polynomial P(q) = ∏_i V̂_i is a product of Markov traces ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.optimal_coeff_eq_transfer_state","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.optimal_coeff_eq_transfer_state","kind":"theorem","line":217,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L217-L254","doc":"**Layer 3 Main Theorem.**\n\nFor each generator σ_i in a nuclear configuration, the coefficient\nof σ_i in the optimal normal form equals the σ-component of the\ntransfer matrix chain state at generator i.\n\nSpecifically, let `seq_i` be the crossing sequence at generator i\n(the crossings that accumulate onto chain i). Then:\n\n- The Hecke element at generator i is `heckeChain (1, 0) seq_i`\n- The transfer matrix chain is `M_i = matrixChain seq_i`\n- The state vector is `M_i · (1, 0)ᵀ = (a_i, b_i)ᵀ`\n- The optimal NF coefficient of σ_i is `a_i`\n- The traced volume is `V̂_i = z · a_i + b_i` (cleared: `a_i + (t+t⁻¹)·b_i`)\n\nThe proof combines:\n1. `chain_correspondence`: Hecke chain = matrix chain on initial state\n2. `traced_vol_from_state`: traced volume = Markov trace of Hecke element\n3. The Markov trace is linear, so per-generator contributions factorise\n\nTogether: the NF coefficients (a_i, b_i) at each generator are\nexactly the entries of the transfer matrix chain state vector,\nand the traced volume V̂_i is the Markov trace of the per-generator\nHecke element. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.traced_vol_from_state","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.traced_vol_from_state","kind":"theorem","line":196,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L196-L211","doc":"The traced volume of a chain matrix, applied to initial state `(1, 0)ᵀ`,\nextracts the Markov trace of the corresponding Hecke element.\n\nIf `M = matrixChain seq`, then:\n`tracedVolCleared M = M[0,0] + (t + t⁻¹) · M[1,0]`\n\nThe state vector is `M · (1, 0)ᵀ = (M[0,0], M[1,0])ᵀ`.\nSo `tracedVolCleared M` computes `a + (t+t⁻¹)·b` where\n`(a, b) = M · (1, 0)ᵀ` is the Hecke state. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.markov_trace_eq_traced_vol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.markov_trace_eq_traced_vol","kind":"theorem","line":180,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L180-L194","doc":"The Markov trace of a Hecke element `a·σ + b·𝟏` is:\n`tr_M(a·σ + b) = a · z + b`\nwhere `z = 1/(t + t⁻¹)` is the Markov parameter.\n\nIn cleared form (multiplied by `t + t⁻¹`):\n`(t + t⁻¹) · tr_M(a·σ + b) = a + (t + t⁻¹) · b`\n\nThis is exactly `tracedVolCleared` applied to the column vector\n`(a, b)ᵀ` viewed as the first column of a matrix. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.chain_correspondence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.chain_correspondence","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L143-L174","doc":"The iterated correspondence: Hecke chain = matrix chain applied to\ninitial state. By induction on the crossing sequence, using\n`hecke_eq_transfer` at each step. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.matrixChain","kind":"def","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L131-L141","doc":"Apply a crossing sequence via transfer matrices, folding from the right.\n\nM = T(cₙ,dₙ) · ⋯ · T(c₂,d₂) · T(c₁,d₁)\n\nThis matches the Hecke chain left-fold: the FIRST crossing in the\nsequence is applied FIRST (innermost in the matrix product).\nSo M.mulVec init = T(cₙ,dₙ) · ⋯ · T(c₁,d₁) · init,\nwhich matches heckeChain evaluating (c₁,d₁) first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.heckeChain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.heckeChain","kind":"def","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L122-L129","doc":"Apply a crossing sequence in the Hecke algebra, folding from the right.\nStarting from state `(a₀, b₀)`, multiply by each `(cⱼ, dⱼ)` in order.\n\nThis corresponds to the product:\n`(a₀·σ + b₀)(c₁·σ + d₁)(c₂·σ + d₂)⋯(cₙ·σ + dₙ)` ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.CrossingSeq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.CrossingSeq","kind":"def","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L118-L120","doc":"A crossing sequence: a list of `(c, d)` pairs representing\nsuccessive Hecke multiplications / transfer matrix applications. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.hecke_eq_transfer","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.hecke_eq_transfer","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L85-L112","doc":"The transfer matrix `T(c, d)` acting on state vector `(a, b)ᵀ`\nproduces the same result as Hecke multiplication of `(a·σ + b)`\nby `(c·σ + d)`.\n\nTransfer matrix action:\n```\nT(c,d) · (a, b)ᵀ = ⎡ c·HA+d,  c ⎤ · ⎡ a ⎤ = ⎡ (c·HA+d)·a + c·b ⎤\n                    ⎣ c,       d ⎦   ⎣ b ⎦   ⎣ c·a + d·b         ⎦\n```\n\nHecke multiplication:\n```\n(a·σ + b)(c·σ + d) = (a·c·HA + a·d + b·c)·σ + (a·c + b·d)·𝟏\n```\n\nThese are equal:\n- Row 0: `(c·HA+d)·a + c·b = a·c·HA + a·d + b·c` ✓ (ring identity)\n- Row 1: `c·a + d·b = a·c + b·d`                   ✓ (commutativity)\n\nThis is the **fundamental correspondence**: the transfer matrix\nencodes exactly the Hecke algebra multiplication with reduction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.heckeMultiply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.heckeMultiply","kind":"def","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L66-L79","doc":"Multiply a Hecke element `(a · σ + b)` by a crossing `(c · σ + d)`\nusing the Hecke relation `σ² = HA · σ + 1`.\n\n`(a · σ + b)(c · σ + d)`\n  `= a·c · σ² + a·d · σ + b·c · σ + b·d`\n  `= a·c · (HA · σ + 1) + (a·d + b·c) · σ + b·d`\n  `= (a·c·HA + a·d + b·c) · σ + (a·c + b·d) · 𝟏`\n\nSo the new state is:\n  `a' = a·c·HA + a·d + b·c`\n  `b' = a·c + b·d`\n","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.identCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.identCoeff","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L59-L60","doc":"Extract the 𝟏-coefficient (identity coefficient) from a state. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.sigmaCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.sigmaCoeff","kind":"def","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L56-L57","doc":"Extract the σ-coefficient (crossing coefficient) from a state. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.initialState","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.initialState","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L50-L54","doc":"The initial state vector `(1, 0)ᵀ`: a bare generator σ_i with\ncoefficient 1 on σ and 0 on 𝟏.  This is the start of a chain\nbefore any further crossings are applied. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.mkState","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.mkState","kind":"def","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L46-L48","doc":"Construct a state vector from crossing and identity coefficients. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.TMCorrespondence.StateVec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.TMCorrespondence.StateVec","kind":"def","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/TMCorrespondence.lean#L40-L44","doc":"A state vector `(a, b)` represents the Hecke element `a · σ_i + b · 𝟏`\nat generator `i`. The correspondence is:\n- component 0: coefficient of σ_i (the crossing term)\n- component 1: coefficient of 𝟏 (the identity term) ","signatureChunk":0}]}